nt":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Each input is a 30×30 mosaic that is almost mirror‑symmetric, both left–right and up–down. The axes are off‑centre: the fact sheet shows that column x matches column 31−x and row y matches row 31−y. One rectangle has been painted over with colour 7, the \"mask\".\n\n**What the output is.** The fact sheet confirms that in every train pair the output has exactly the size of the 7‑rectangle's bounding box. So the output is the patch of mosaic that the 7s are hiding. This is a completion puzzle.\n\n**How to recover a hidden cell (y, x).** Because both symmetries hold together, each cell has up to three partners with the same value: (y, 31−x), (31−y, x) and (31−y, 31−x). We take the first partner that is inside the grid and not itself covered by 7.\n\nSometimes all three partners are unusable. For example, in pair 1 the mask touches column 0, whose left–right partner would be column 31 (outside the grid). Its up–down partner may also be masked. For such a cell I fall back to the weak transpose cue (x, y) and that cell's three mirror partners. The fact sheet rates this cue as a last resort only, so it is used only when nothing else works.\n\n**Construction stages.**\n1. Start from the input.\n2. Repair the masked region in place, writing each 7 cell's partner value.\n3. Crop the repaired region to the 7's bounding box. That crop is the answer.\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The mask is a 10×2 strip at rows 16–25, columns 19–20. The left–right partners are columns 12 and 11, which lie well inside the grid and are not masked. So the strip is filled straight from its mirror partners, and the output is a 10×2 grid.\n\n**Status.** I have not run this code on the train pairs yet; the harness check is the first real test. The transpose fallback is the least certain part. If the harness reports mismatches, they are most likely in cells that needed that fallback.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    emit(g.copy(), \"Initial canvas: a doubly mirror-symmetric mosaic with a rectangle masked by colour 7.\")\n    mask = g == 7\n    ys, xs = np.where(mask)\n    y0, y1, x0, x1 = ys.min(), ys.max(), xs.min(), xs.max()\n    N = 31  # mirror: x -> 31-x, y -> 31-y\n\n    def ok(y, x):\n        return 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W and not mask[y, x]\n\n    fixed = g.copy()\n    for y in range(y0, y1 + 1):\n        for x in range(x0, x1 + 1):\n            if not mask[y, x]:\n                continue\n            val = None\n            for (a, b) in [(y, N - x), (N - y, x), (N - y, N - x)]:\n                if ok(a, b):\n                    val = g[a, b]\n                    break\n            if val is None:\n                for (a, b) in [(x, y), (x, N - y), (N - x, y), (N - x, N - y)]:\n                    if ok(a, b):\n                        val = g[a, b]\n                        break\n            if val is not None:\n                fixed[y, x] = val\n    emit(fixed.copy(), \"Repaired the masked rectangle: each 7 cell takes the value of its mirror partner (y,31-x), (31-y,x) or (31-y,31-x).\")\n    out = fixed[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1].copy()\n    emit(out, \"Cropped the repaired region (the bounding box of the 7 mask) as the answer.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the grid is made of.** Each input is a 30×30 mosaic that is almost mirror‑symmetric, both left–right and up–down."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How to recover a hidden cell (y, x).** Because both symmetries hold together, each cell has up to three partners with the same value: (y, 31−x), (31−y, x) and (31−y, 31−x)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"For such a cell I fall back to the weak transpose cue (x, y) and that cell's three mirror partners."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"For such a cell I fall back to the weak transpose cue (x, y) and that cell's three mirror partners."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"e fact sheet confirms that in every train pair the output has exactly the size of the 7‑rectangle's bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Crop the repaired region to the 7's bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"That crop is the answer."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"367c4b36e41095966554e62e91aedfe45f643304042ac86720a54f5aff77bc91","task_sha256":"5096edc473407a61d23d758949c528dca5a9f9ad2dc333952e7a754d3f2df514","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
